## Recall Rundown

### Allergen Missteps

One of the key recalls this week involves allergen mislabeling. J and J Bunting, Inc mislabeled their "Sesame Seeds White" as "Curry Powder Sweet," and Market of Choice Vegan Kale Caesar Salad failed to account for sesame seeds as a sub-ingredient in their Za'atar spice mix. These oversights could have severe consequences for consumers with allergies, and remind us of the importance of meticulous labeling processes.

### Salmonella Strikes Again

Salmonella contamination has made yet another appearance, with JCB Flavors, LLC implicated in multiple recalls for products ranging from White Cheddar Seasoning to Dill & Garlic Seasoning. The Coffee Connexion Co., Inc and Nassar Investment Company LLC also reported Salmonella contamination in cheese sauces and tahini, respectively. Given the severity of illnesses caused by Salmonella, this trend raises red flags about preventive measures in production.

### Foreign Objects and More

Straus Family Creamery had two products recalled due to potential contamination with metal pieces, and Sato Pharmaceutical Inc. faced a recall for its Yunker Energy & Health Herbal Supplement failing to meet Vitamin E label claims.

## Repeat Offenders

Notably, brands such as Wellnov Supplements LLC and Albertsons Companies LLC remain in the spotlight for frequent recalls, with 14 and 11 recalls respectively under their belts this year alone. Moonlight Packing Corporation takes the dubious honor of being the worst offender with 11 Class I recalls in 2026.

## By the Numbers

This year, the FDA has recorded a total of 543 recalls, with 173 classified as Class I (indicating significant health risks). This highlights a concerning trend in manufacturing and supply chain oversight.

### Monthly Recall Trends

The trend for monthly recalls shows a spike in May 2026 with 173 recalls, followed by a noticeable decrease in June with 43 so far.

### Categorizing Risks

Looking at recall reasons, allergens top the list with 256 cases this year, followed by Salmonella contamination at 172 cases, and foreign objects at 98 cases, painting a clear picture of the prevalent issues.

## Final Thoughts

Consumers should remain vigilant about checking labels and staying informed on recalls. Brands must uphold higher standards to ensure food safety. As recall numbers rise, so does responsibility.
